WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.893
Python applications often sit behind frameworks such as Flask, Django, and
FastAPI.

2
00:00:04.893 --> 00:00:12.233
Those frameworks receive request bodies, query strings, path parameters,
headers, cookies, form fields, and uploaded files.

3
00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:15.902
Each of those values can be controlled or influenced by a client.

4
00:00:15.902 --> 00:00:23.514
The application should validate what the server receives, not what the
browser or API documentation suggested a client should send.

5
00:00:23.514 --> 00:00:30.989
JSON payloads are common in Python APIs, but JSON parsing only tells the
application that the payload is syntactically valid JSON.

6
00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:40.640
It does not prove that required fields exist, that values have safe
lengths, that nested objects match expectations, or that the data is
authorized for the current user.

7
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.290
Query strings and headers have the same issue.

8
00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:47.164
They may parse correctly while still violating application rules.

9
00:00:47.164 --> 00:00:50.630
Python programs also process input outside web frameworks.

10
00:00:50.630 --> 00:01:01.639
Command-line arguments, environment variables, configuration files, YAML,
XML, CSV, database records, message queues, and scheduled job payloads all
carry data across trust boundaries.

11
00:01:01.639 --> 00:01:11.765
A batch job or internal admin script may feel safer than a public
endpoint, but it can still process malformed imports, stale records, or
data from a compromised upstream system.

12
00:01:11.765 --> 00:01:15.910
A practical validation strategy begins by inventorying input sources.

13
00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:25.697
For each source, ask who controls the data, how it is encoded, how it is
parsed, what structure is expected, what business rule applies, and where
the value will be used next.

14
00:01:25.697 --> 00:01:33.240
That map helps the team place validation close to the boundary and avoid
treating internal plumbing as automatically trustworthy.
